35,2,1,talk,Ryuji,,"Transport costs are the biggest variable, spiking significantly when we travel further. Also, whole-building lets are risky because non-refundable deposits mean the remaining members pay for any last-minute cancellations.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Toshio,,"Public bookings are a bureaucratic nightmare with a six-month lottery and rigid paperwork. Furthermore, cooking for ourselves is a massive time sink that takes hours away from actual training.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Minako,,"The seniors believe that mixing year-groups only happens during the evenings when everyone stays together. However, the attendance sheets show that a fifth of our members didn't even attend the away camps.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Jonathan,,"The public beds are quite affordable, but other clubs who went inland reported sleeping eight people to a room with very strict lights-out policies. I worry that such crowded conditions might hinder our rest.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Asuka,,"The attendance sheets confirm a worrying trend of high dropout rates. Combined with the non-refundable deposits for whole-building lets, we risk placing an unfair financial burden on the members who actually attend.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Ryuji,,"@Asuka, I agree. If we choose a private lodge and people drop out, the cost per person will spike. We cannot afford to miscalculate the budget again.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Ryuji, budget is important, but we must also consider the organizers' time. If we choose a place that requires us to cook and handle endless paperwork, we're just trading financial risk for burnout.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Minako,,"If we prioritize the budget and the organizers' time, we might sacrifice the social health of the club. If we don't stay together in the evenings, we won't solve the issue of the year-groups remaining isolated.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Minako, I understand the need for bonding, but we must ensure the environment allows for actual recovery. If we are too crowded or exhausted, the social atmosphere may suffer regardless.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Minako, bonding is valuable, but it shouldn't come at the cost of financial instability. If we choose a destination that forces a few members to subsidize those who don't show up, it will create resentment, not unity.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Ryuji,,"@Toshio, I understand the burnout risk, but financial instability is a more permanent problem. If we stay local, we eliminate the transport spike and the risk of non-refundable deposits. It's the only way to guarantee a stable fee.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Toshio,,"Staying local might solve the budget, but if the canteen is closed, we're back to the cooking duty problem. We can't ignore the administrative burden on the few people who actually do the work.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Minako,,"If we stay local, members will likely go home at night. We've already seen that a fifth of the club doesn't attend away camps; if we remove the overnight element, we risk losing the club's cohesion entirely.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Jonathan,,"I agree that cohesion is important, but I am concerned that poor sleep quality can be just as damaging. If we are crowded into rooms of eight, we may be too tired to bond effectively.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Toshio, the administrative burden is a concern, but it is manageable compared to the risk of financial deficits. We must prioritize a system where the costs are predictable and fair for every member.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Ryuji,,"@Minako, cohesion is a goal, but it cannot be built on a shaky financial foundation. If we prioritize the overnight experience over budget stability, we risk repeating last year's deficit. Local is the only rational choice.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Toshio,,"If we prioritize the budget over everything, we're ignoring the human cost. A ""rational"" choice that burns out the organizers and forces them into hours of cooking is not sustainable for the club's future.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Minako,,"We must look at the long-term survival of the club. Financials and logistics are important, but if we lose our members to isolation and dropout, we won't have a club to budget for. We need a destination that ensures we stay together.","{""words"":42,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Jonathan,,"It seems we are balancing three different pressures: budget, workload, and social health. However, we must not forget the physical toll. If we prioritize bonding but sacrifice sleep, we risk the quality of our training.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Asuka,,"We are discussing different priorities, but the financial risk is the most concrete. If we choose a high-risk option and the dropout rate persists, the remaining members will be unfairly penalized. We need a stable, predictable cost.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
