60,3,1,talk,Ryuji,,Option B is the only choice that eliminates the transport spike and the risk of non-refundable deposits. It provides the financial stability we need to avoid another deficit.,"{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Toshio,,"Option B is a risk for the organizers. If the canteen is closed, we're stuck with the cooking duties that burned people out last year. Option A removes that burden entirely.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Minako,,"Option A is the most effective for the club's social health. By staying together in a private building, we ensure the evening bonding that the seniors highlighted as essential for mixing the year-groups.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Jonathan,,"Option C seems risky given the reports of crowded rooms and strict rules. If we are eight to a room, the lack of quality sleep will likely undermine our training and well-being.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Asuka,,"Option A is financially reckless. With non-refundable deposits and a history of high dropout rates, the remaining members would likely end up subsidizing those who don't attend. Option B is the only stable choice.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Ryuji,,"@Toshio, the cooking burden is a temporary inconvenience, but a financial deficit is a lasting liability. Option B is the only way to ensure we don't announce a fee that we can't actually cover.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Ryuji, it is not a ""temporary inconvenience"" when it costs hours of training every day and burns out the staff. We cannot expect the same people to sacrifice their time again. Option A is the only sustainable choice for the organizers.","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Minako,,"@Ryuji, @Asuka, while I understand the financial concerns, we cannot ignore the dropout rate. If we choose Option B, the lack of a shared overnight experience will only accelerate the isolation of our members. We need the Coastal Lodge to save the club's spirit.","{""words"":44,""mentions"":[""Ryuji"",""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Minako, I appreciate the desire to save the club's spirit, but spirit cannot be maintained if the members are physically exhausted. Option A's distance and Option C's crowding both threaten our recovery. Option B seems the most peaceful.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Minako, the ""spirit"" of the club cannot be built on resentment. If we choose Option A and the cost spikes due to dropouts, the members who actually show up will be the ones paying the price. That is not a sustainable way to foster unity.","{""words"":45,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Ryuji,,"@Jonathan, I'm glad we agree on the stability of Option B. @Toshio, we can find a way to manage the cooking or find alternative meals, but we cannot simply ignore the risk of a budget shortfall. I vote for Option B.","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[""Jonathan"",""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Toshio,,"@Ryuji, ""finding a way"" is exactly how we ended up with burnout last year. We need a solution that doesn't rely on the sacrifice of a few. I vote for Option A.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Minako,,"@Asuka, @Ryuji, the financial risk is real, but the risk of the club dissolving due to a lack of cohesion is greater. We must prioritize the members' bonds. I vote for Option A.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Asuka"",""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Jonathan,,"I believe Option B is the most prudent choice. It avoids the crowding of the Mountain Centre and the financial instability of the Lodge, allowing us to focus on our training in a calm environment. I vote for Option B.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Minako, prioritizing bonds is noble, but not when it creates a financial penalty for the most committed members. Option B is the only logical choice that ensures fairness and stability. I vote for Option B.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Ryuji,,I have stated my position. Option B is the only rational way to protect the club's accounts and ensure no member is unfairly penalized. I stand by my vote for Option B.,"{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Toshio,,"The administrative burden is a real cost, not just a detail. If we choose Option B, we are choosing to burn out our organizers again. I stand by my vote for Option A.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Minako,,"The long-term survival of the club depends on the bonds between members, not just the balance of a ledger. If we lose our cohesion, we lose everything. I stand by my vote for Option A.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Jonathan,,"I wish for everyone to find a balance between their needs, but for the sake of our health and stability, I believe Option B is the most serene choice. I stand by my vote for Option B.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Asuka,,"The financial risk of Option A is too high to ignore, and Option C is unsuitable for recovery. Option B is the only choice that guarantees fairness and stability for all members. I stand by my vote for Option B.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
